  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">A transcript records what was said. The minutes record what was decided.</p>
    <p>
      After every council meeting the district office writes the official minutes: every resolution with its vote, the
      statements that belong on record, who was present, who left the room and when, and who has to sign. The
      transcription the office had commissioned delivers the words. The minutes were still written in Word, by listening
      again and checking every resolution against a separate resolution record.
    </p>
    <p>
      I care about this kind of problem because it is the one Levelwork works on: turning a conversation into a record
      that people can check and sign off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the office told us</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Nobody had time to check a transcript line by line. They needed the minutes.</p>
    <p>
      On 30 September we sat down with the office and went through a real meeting in the tool. Four things changed the
      plan. The person who writes the minutes had no time to proofread every line; what mattered was getting to the
      minutes fast. Jumping from a line of text into the recording was the feature they used most. A marked pause in a
      council meeting almost always means something was not captured. And the office’s management set the goal: press
      export once and have the finished minutes in Word, in the office’s own template.
    </p>
    <p>
      We had been commissioned for the verbatim record. After that meeting we moved the focus to the minutes, and I
      designed the minutes editor. Eighteen points came out of that meeting; on 16 October the export to Word and PDF,
      the synced preview and the phone layout went live.
    </p>
  </section>

