  <section>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">The machine prepares, a person decides, and nothing leaves the building.</p>
    <figure>
      <AmbactaPipeline />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Michael’s platform records, transcribes and names the speakers on a server in the office’s own building. Its rules
      hold everywhere: the language model never changes a proper name, names are corrected by the dictionary or by a
      person; votes are never filled in by a model, they come word for word from the resolution record. My part starts
      where the office takes over: speech sorted into the agenda items from the meeting documents, the editor with one
      decision per contribution, and the export into the office’s template with a “draft” watermark until it is signed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What didn’t work</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">One real meeting, all real documents, and an export nobody touched.</p>
    <p>
      On 15 October I ran the public council meeting of 18 May 2026 through the editor with everything the office uses:
      the record of the 2 h 39 min recording, about 17,000 words, the resolution record and the office’s template. I left
      the export unedited and compared it, item by item, with what the office needs. The resolutions came through
      complete and word for word, with votes and numbering. Most other things broke somewhere, and most were fixed the
      same day.
    </p>
