  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Choices instead of typing, documents instead of retyping, and the page always in view.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>A choice per contribution.</strong> A free text editor would have been quicker to build. But the office
      does not rewrite the meeting; for each contribution it decides what the minutes need: edit, keep verbatim, leave
      out, replace with a standard text, or indirect speech, which is still being built. Choices make that decision fast
      and leave a trace of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>No rich-text library.</strong> Bold, italic and underline are a small markup string, not an editable HTML
      field. The state has to sync into a second window, go through undo, and break into Word’s own text runs; a text
      editor library would have fought all three.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Read the documents, say how sure we are.</strong> The resolution record arrives as Word or PDF. The editor
      reads both, shows what it found, and warns when a PDF had to be reconstructed from the page layout.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The minutes stay in view.</strong> Editing and preview scroll together, so the office always sees the page
      that will be signed, not a form that becomes a page later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Name, brand and website</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">“Serves the office. Stays on premises.” I gave the product its name and its face.</p>
    <p>
      Inside the team it was called Nautilus, a name that did not carry outside. The first candidate, Hoheit, fell
      through: the domains were taken, and it sounds as if the AI held authority itself. Ambacta goes back to ambactus,
      Caesar’s word for a man in another’s service, the root of the German “Amt”, office. The name sets the order: the AI
      serves, the office decides. That is the worry staff councils bring to every AI purchase, so the name answers it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I built three design directions, Clear, Glass and File, and chose Glass, so that website and app look like one
      piece. Deliberately absent: eagles, crests and seals, because German law forbids signs that can be confused with
      state emblems. The website shows the app with a fictional session and lets visitors try the review themselves.
    </p>
